09-22T10:51:36Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T10:51:36Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test: green the Cypress suite (stale upload assertions + false dependency-tab premise)\n\nTakes the Cypress suite from 5 failing to 0: `All specs passed! 137 137`.\n\nNeither failure came from the SEC-271 security work (#39) — that commit touches\nno frontend file. Two unrelated causes, both predating it.\n\nSpec 02, three assertions stale since `ba5e9d8 \"fix: allow to upload tgz\nmodules\"` (2026-07-13); the spec was last updated 2026-06-17 and still asserted\nthe pre-tgz dialog:\n\n  ''Deploy single module (.jar)''   ->  ''Deploy single module (.jar / .tgz)''\n  ''Drag & drop a .jar file here''  ->  ''Drag & drop a .jar or .tgz file here, …''\n  accept=''.jar''                   ->  accept=''.jar,.tgz''\n\n`cy.contains` is a substring match and ''Deploy single module (.jar)'' is not a\nsubstring of ''… (.jar / .tgz)'' because of the closing paren, which is why it\nfailed rather than silently still matching. The tgz support was deliberate, so\nthe assertions move, not the dialog.\n\nSpec 03 presented as flakiness and was not: the tests asserted a tab that cannot\nrender. The block claimed module-management-community declares a Jahia module\ndependency on graphql-dxm-provider, but that is a Maven `provided` dependency —\ncompile-time only. The built manifest carries no Jahia-Depends header and\nrequires no com.jahia.modules.dependencies capability, so\nGqlBundle#getModuleDependencyGraph — which walks exactly that namespace —\nproduces no ''-->'' edges and BundleDetails correctly hides the tab. The component\nwas right; the test was wrong.\n\nOne of the three used to pass only by accident: openDetailsFor typed into the\ndebounced filter and immediately clicked .first(), so it could open a DIFFERENT\nbundle''s dialog — one that did have Jahia module dependencies. Failure\nscreenshots show the filter correctly reduced to one row with no dialog open at\nall, i.e. a click swallowed by the re-render.\n\nFixed by borrowing spec 01''s sequence (it passes 65/65): settle the table on\n''Showing 1 to 1 of 1 modules'' and the row button before clicking, and assert the\ndialog''s CONTENT rather than its mere presence so the wrong bundle''s dialog can\nno longer read as a pass. The tab query is a single cy.contains(selector,\ncontent), which has no captured parent to go stale. The three tests now target\n\"Bundle dependencies\" (osgi.wiring.package), serving the same purpose — a graph\ntab that activates and exposes the Mermaid render with its WCAG AAA text\nalternative — without needing a module dependency the bundle does not have.\nRuntime drops from ~3-5 min to ~1 min.\n\nSecond commit gives the harness its own COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME. Six of eight\nharnesses here derive the project from the directory name `tests` and so share\none namespace, where `docker compose down --remove-orphans` adopts and destroys\nthe others'' containers — it removed the privateappstore rig''s nexus on\n2026-09-22. clamav-filter and mfa-community-parent already set it.\n\nVerified on an isolated stack against a jar confirmed to carry the SEC-271 fix:\nspec 01 65/0, spec 02 51/0, spec 03 21/0. Lint unchanged at 4 for\n02-uploadDownload and 165 for 03-features, two below its 167 baseline.\n\nRefs: https://github.com/Jahia/jahia-security-scan/issues/565","tree":{"sha":"ef68a79b4abbc4aec6c00f5c3a6b676ab9e2328c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/module-management-community/git/trees/ef68a79b4abbc4aec6c00f5c3a6b676ab9e2328c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/module-management-community/git/commits/21227a1599a4e55fdd9874f0d7fd9ed42d68e5b2","verification":{"payload":"tree ef68a79b4abbc4aec6c00f5c3a6b676ab9e2328c\nparent 93b329fb0dab56efa913623906f9a5a830091a89\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1790074296 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790074296 +0200\n\ntest: green the Cypress suite (stale upload assertions + false dependency-tab premise)\n\nTakes the Cypress suite from 5 failing to 0: `All specs passed! 137 137`.\n\nNeither failure came from the SEC-271 security work (#39) — that commit touches\nno frontend file. Two unrelated causes, both predating it.\n\nSpec 02, three assertions stale since `ba5e9d8 \"fix: allow to upload tgz\nmodules\"` (2026-07-13); the spec was last updated 2026-06-17 and still asserted\nthe pre-tgz dialog:\n\n  ''Deploy single module (.jar)''   ->  ''Deploy single module (.jar / .tgz)''\n  ''Drag & drop a .jar file here''  ->  ''Drag & drop a .jar or .tgz file here, …''\n  accept=''.jar''                   ->  accept=''.jar,.tgz''\n\n`cy.contains` is a substring match and ''Deploy single module (.jar)'' is not a\nsubstring of ''… (.jar / .tgz)'' because of the closing paren, which is why it\nfailed rather than silently still matching. The tgz support was deliberate, so\nthe assertions move, not the dialog.\n\nSpec 03 presented as flakiness and was not: the tests asserted a tab that cannot\nrender. The block claimed module-management-community declares a Jahia module\ndependency on graphql-dxm-provider, but that is a Maven `provided` dependency —\ncompile-time only. The built manifest carries no Jahia-Depends header and\nrequires no com.jahia.modules.dependencies capability, so\nGqlBundle#getModuleDependencyGraph — which walks exactly that namespace —\nproduces no ''-->'' edges and BundleDetails correctly hides the tab. The component\nwas right; the test was wrong.\n\nOne of the three used to pass only by accident: openDetailsFor typed into the\ndebounced filter and immediately clicked .first(), so it could open a DIFFERENT\nbundle''s dialog — one that did have Jahia module dependencies. Failure\nscreenshots show the filter correctly reduced to one row with no dialog open at\nall, i.e. a click swallowed by the re-render.\n\nFixed by borrowing spec 01''s sequence (it passes 65/65): settle the table on\n''Showing 1 to 1 of 1 modules'' and the row button before clicking, and assert the\ndialog''s CONTENT rather than its mere presence so the wrong bundle''s dialog can\nno longer read as a pass. The tab query is a single cy.contains(selector,\ncontent), which has no captured parent to go stale. The three tests now target\n\"Bundle dependencies\" (osgi.wiring.package), serving the same purpose — a graph\ntab that activates and exposes the Mermaid render with its WCAG AAA text\nalternative — without needing a module dependency the bundle does not have.\nRuntime drops from ~3-5 min to ~1 min.\n\nSecond commit gives the harness its own COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME. Six of eight\nharnesses here derive the project from the directory name `tests` and so share\none namespace, where `docker compose down --remove-orphans` adopts and destroys\nthe others'' containers — it removed the privateappstore rig''s nexus on\n2026-09-22. clamav-filter and mfa-community-parent already set it.\n\nVerified on an isolated stack against a jar confirmed to carry the SEC-271 fix:\nspec 01 65/0, spec 02 51/0, spec 03 21/0. Lint unchanged at 4 for\n02-uploadDownload and 165 for 03-features, two below its 167 baseline.\n\nRefs: https://github.com/Jahia/jahia-security-scan/issues/565","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsl24CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAAnAQAAc3iAbvtigEHHnFvyAOO2HZ\nShZGD6/F4XKY87scfLDTg5tuqrkieC+YNkQMZ4a1x1Aj5XY0twk29/HbZsLUnC5U\nLMY/eOa48JklDGMx8LAH5TUMDFeSRO1MFKT9wVfATypvmbDGiFcnTgcl+xqo+Vyi\nM/RwapehgYFWYLbA4bQLiAR5I2c68BsOyENDuNTQTQ6gk5aJVpYS7IXWXbHm5Wgt\nfTXqiPnak0BvyOIHXUeQEclrSl+cu+8XGm8YOPk/hIVKPrg4XJpOI2NQJ2ufGxwn\nhjBC2vFRQuIsmJ3g6Y48rnjFzowp4XY1LDvq8xh0fIDtbULTgdwrUSyzuZS5pTl8\n88tdXFd9Zgmlng751IBjo3JgK9Rj2vmCQgqfpaNMTXZFFx175tZdJETRDvhjVMNw\n00znHUNFkTzIYiU8vp9tOXx6JsegkU92krInX2IcoEE0Qwu0i4b9UuQj440DpTrT\njY5c4ELr2y/7CQE8lKcTT08ocUQ2CzudDuAdEbpQL2SWquY1jx2w43/GxTE8xwvw\nB3sR3kf2pl05Gm8AIMfsSmTHjqdeb7v50cGVrjDmWOom2vzZludO97At8Q+RNGCz\nPQLcDFIYGlWJRFLJk0OUrQS836WAOVR0QEfI3iC4fQyt7q8+j719S7FvsDqPlbZx\nBsjzzNypLhcgGeJfu6tF\n=8UzU\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T10:51:37Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/module-management-community/commit/21227a1599a4e55fdd9874f0d7fd9ed42d68e5b2","node_id":"C_kwDOOy14NtoAKDIxMjI3YTE1OTlhNGU1NWZkZDk4NzRmMGQ3ZmQ5ZWQ0MmQ2OGU1YjI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/module-management-community/commit/93b329fb0dab56efa913623906f9a5a830091a89","sha":"93b329fb0dab56efa913623906f9a5a830091a89","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/module-management-community/commits/93b329fb0dab56efa913623906f9a5a830091a89"}],"sha":"21227a1599a4e55fdd9874f0d7fd9ed42d68e5b2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/module-management-community/commits/21227a1599a4e55fdd9874f0d7fd9ed42d68e5b2"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/module-management-community/commits/93b329fb0dab56efa913623906f9a5a830091a89/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-22T07:54:30Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-22T07:54:30Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: SSRF egress filter on the store module index URL (SEC-271)\n\n`storeModuleListUrl` is an OSGi configuration value that the Jahia JVM fetches, and\n`refreshStoreIndex()` passed it straight to `new URL(...).openConnection()` with no scheme, host or\naddress validation. The component declares `configurationPolicy = REQUIRE` with no `@Modified`\nmethod, so Felix SCR deactivates and reactivates it on any write to its `.cfg`, and `activate()`\nunconditionally schedules a refresh on both branches — a config write alone became an outbound\nrequest from the server. JAHIA-SEC-271 proved it live: a captured inbound GET from `Java/17.0.19`\n~0.9s after the write, no admin action in between.\n\nThe delivery half of that chain was closed in osgi-configurations-manager#28; the fiche is explicit\nthat the sink here was left untouched. This implements its `remediation.risk_notes` item (1).\n\n`StoreIndexUrlValidator` admits https only, refuses embedded credentials, and refuses hosts\nresolving into loopback, wildcard, link-local (the 169.254.169.254 metadata endpoint), site-local,\nIPv6 unique-local, carrier-grade-NAT (Alibaba''s 100.100.100.200), multicast, class-E and 6to4 relay\nanycast, plus a denylist of internal host names checked before resolution. IPv6 transition forms are\njudged by the IPv4 they carry: the deprecated wrappers (IPv4-compatible, 6to4, Teredo) and the\nlocal-use NAT64 prefix are refused outright, while the well-known `64:ff9b::/96` and the\nIPv4-translated `::ffff:0:0/96` are unwrapped and re-run through the full policy — blanket-refusing\nNAT64 would stop an IPv6-only node reaching the legitimate store, a functional regression dressed up\nas a fix. All-digit host labels with a leading zero are refused because `0177` is decimal to this JVM\nand octal to an `inet_aton` proxy, which would make the validator''s verdict describe a different\nhost than the one contacted.\n\n`StoreIndexHttpFetcher` turns off `setInstanceFollowRedirects` and follows redirects itself,\nre-checking every hop: validating only the configured URL would leave an allowed host free to answer\n`302 Location: http://169.254.169.254/...`, which HttpURLConnection follows on its own.\n\nComponent activation runs only the resolver-free checks, so a `.cfg` naming a host whose resolver\nhangs cannot stall the SCR thread; the address checks run on the asynchronous refresh path,\nimmediately before the socket opens. A rejected value is logged and replaced by the shipped default,\nso update detection degrades to the bundled catalogue rather than failing. An unresolvable host is\nstill refused — accepting it would be a free DNS-rebinding primitive — but at WARN rather than ERROR\nso an offline node does not emit an error on every scheduled refresh.\n\nThe policy is deliberately not an OSGi property: the attacker''s primitive rewrites that whole file,\nso an allow-list living in it would defend nothing. Operators running an internal mirror use the\nsystem property `jahia.modulemanagement.storeIndex.allowInternalHosts`, which an OSGi configuration\nwrite cannot set.\n\nResidual gaps are documented rather than claimed closed, in the class javadoc and the README: DNS\nrebinding (bounded in practice by the JDK''s 30s positive cache and by TLS hostname verification\nreducing it to a blind connect probe, both deployment-dependent), NAT64 network-specific prefixes\nwhich cannot be enumerated, forward proxies, and the escape hatch not being an absolute boundary.\nThis filter also constrains where the request goes, not what comes back — what an attacker-controlled\ncatalogue can do is tracked separately as SEC-435.\n\nBREAKING CHANGE: a `storeModuleListUrl` pointing at an internal mirror (private IP, `localhost`, or\nplain http) is now rejected and falls back to the public store URL. Restore it with\n`-Djahia.modulemanagement.storeIndex.allowInternalHosts=true` on the Jahia JVM. Installations on the\ndefault `https://store.jahia.com/...` URL are unaffected.\n\nTests: 66 unit tests, the repo''s first. They include a replay of the fiche''s own proof-of-concept —\nloopback listener, policy in force, zero hits — paired with a positive control proving an\nunvalidated fetch does reach that same listener, without which the \"zero hits\" assertion would pass\njust as happily when the listener never started.\n\nRefs: https://github.com/Jahia/jahia-security-scan/issues/565\nRefs: SECURITY-865","tree":{"sha":"bec06b0235f9a60f1ba67fff9041980ed6138384","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/module-management-community/git/trees/bec06b0235f9a60f1ba67fff9041980ed6138384"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/module-management-community/git/commits/93b329fb0dab56efa913623906f9a5a830091a89","verification":{"payload":"tree bec06b0235f9a60f1ba67fff9041980ed6138384\nparent 016d22551c140cb6bf9449cd0fe41384bfa45c52\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1790063670 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790063670 +0200\n\nfix: SSRF egress filter on the store module index URL (SEC-271)\n\n`storeModuleListUrl` is an OSGi configuration value that the Jahia JVM fetches, and\n`refreshStoreIndex()` passed it straight to `new URL(...).openConnection()` with no scheme, host or\naddress validation. The component declares `configurationPolicy = REQUIRE` with no `@Modified`\nmethod, so Felix SCR deactivates and reactivates it on any write to its `.cfg`, and `activate()`\nunconditionally schedules a refresh on both branches — a config write alone became an outbound\nrequest from the server. JAHIA-SEC-271 proved it live: a captured inbound GET from `Java/17.0.19`\n~0.9s after the write, no admin action in between.\n\nThe delivery half of that chain was closed in osgi-configurations-manager#28; the fiche is explicit\nthat the sink here was left untouched. This implements its `remediation.risk_notes` item (1).\n\n`StoreIndexUrlValidator` admits https only, refuses embedded credentials, and refuses hosts\nresolving into loopback, wildcard, link-local (the 169.254.169.254 metadata endpoint), site-local,\nIPv6 unique-local, carrier-grade-NAT (Alibaba''s 100.100.100.200), multicast, class-E and 6to4 relay\nanycast, plus a denylist of internal host names checked before resolution. IPv6 transition forms are\njudged by the IPv4 they carry: the deprecated wrappers (IPv4-compatible, 6to4, Teredo) and the\nlocal-use NAT64 prefix are refused outright, while the well-known `64:ff9b::/96` and the\nIPv4-translated `::ffff:0:0/96` are unwrapped and re-run through the full policy — blanket-refusing\nNAT64 would stop an IPv6-only node reaching the legitimate store, a functional regression dressed up\nas a fix. All-digit host labels with a leading zero are refused because `0177` is decimal to this JVM\nand octal to an `inet_aton` proxy, which would make the validator''s verdict describe a different\nhost than the one contacted.\n\n`StoreIndexHttpFetcher` turns off `setInstanceFollowRedirects` and follows redirects itself,\nre-checking every hop: validating only the configured URL would leave an allowed host free to answer\n`302 Location: http://169.254.169.254/...`, which HttpURLConnection follows on its own.\n\nComponent activation runs only the resolver-free checks, so a `.cfg` naming a host whose resolver\nhangs cannot stall the SCR thread; the address checks run on the asynchronous refresh path,\nimmediately before the socket opens. A rejected value is logged and replaced by the shipped default,\nso update detection degrades to the bundled catalogue rather than failing. An unresolvable host is\nstill refused — accepting it would be a free DNS-rebinding primitive — but at WARN rather than ERROR\nso an offline node does not emit an error on every scheduled refresh.\n\nThe policy is deliberately not an OSGi property: the attacker''s primitive rewrites that whole file,\nso an allow-list living in it would defend nothing. Operators running an internal mirror use the\nsystem property `jahia.modulemanagement.storeIndex.allowInternalHosts`, which an OSGi configuration\nwrite cannot set.\n\nResidual gaps are documented rather than claimed closed, in the class javadoc and the README: DNS\nrebinding (bounded in practice by the JDK''s 30s positive cache and by TLS hostname verification\nreducing it to a blind connect probe, both deployment-dependent), NAT64 network-specific prefixes\nwhich cannot be enumerated, forward proxies, and the escape hatch not being an absolute boundary.\nThis filter also constrains where the request goes, not what comes back — what an attacker-controlled\ncatalogue can do is tracked separately as SEC-435.\n\nBREAKING CHANGE: a `storeModuleListUrl` pointing at an internal mirror (private IP, `localhost`, or\nplain http) is now rejected and falls back to the public store URL. Restore it with\n`-Djahia.modulemanagement.storeIndex.allowInternalHosts=true` on the Jahia JVM. Installations on the\ndefault `https://store.jahia.com/...` URL are unaffected.\n\nTests: 66 unit tests, the repo''s first. They include a replay of the fiche''s own proof-of-concept —\nloopback listener, policy in force, zero hits — paired with a positive control proving an\nunvalidated fetch does reach that same listener, without which the \"zero hits\" assertion would pass\njust as happily when the listener never started.\n\nRefs: https://github.com/Jahia/jahia-security-scan/issues/565\nRefs: SECURITY-865","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsjQ2CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAApwUQAHQbnzVEXjDSjkGCN0D/It8c\nIXkdn25Q3bUFk9/Tj7ouzvLZ6puOSJyvejvInoXkncYf7164y/z2f1Y/C1xnk3rz\njv8liwoUQ6u59gw2oTnyGXduEFS9hSWiCWOxWl92GLrBTGwFHFi9VTIwXkXcGbGb\n+FYokEW3CVwJw0REdFV9Ukm4kIMY0ppoxDZ+Yh/9F6YRgb0CjmcfvsaTQwOizZt0\n6HcDfwfOZgjUqGy52szcDnhTTEuLIsm8n5SdQV6z9kczGQ2kOGkZJv8eV4YWPviB\neNoAgnurD+QQxYybq3c8gr+KyyDpwoO8K8UV6tGjP5L4xlWahN6VndjyPmlcK5/q\n5aws+Jlbc2KWocj7QFskUEXSlEhFb0ugtsGnAYNrG3ZAyEVUgRA/+96X9FftscOr\nVPxa2SmJ8uGJimAK1gObVquwQEjvEhFWo5Y7sCItyS38PSpY/1Ke7i2AS1G4jFXu\nIOixkqgFzLneEVsecG+BLzt1bGmiErdx6eAlQXNlz7h31nu8S2oBcQqK2p9J93ZM\nix9yuvIpHhCfROUYbvbrXA9kx3KS9kByzjndNpFCknOTIZx5bvdpq++l/mVvNn3/\nd2P9cYI8aIx5opU7cPoSYCTqjIWJxWGk9RuTs/a5nYkV7SIjZ52RrQXaeZ6swpsy\nEgdZLKsvuNJRz3IsyC6t\n=DJ7N\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T07:54:31Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url"